= Local Organizing

Elections are won in union halls, grange meetings and church basements long before they are won at the ballot box. Where should our organizers spend the month?

- @organize_farmers: Among the plains farmers and their co-operatives.
- @organize_urban: In the immigrant wards of the cities.
- @organize_industrial: In the mine, mill and packing-house towns.
- @organize_towns: In the small towns.
- @organize_none: Nowhere in particular; let the month pass.

@organize_farmers
on-arrival: farmers_fl += 2

Grain elevator meetings, county fair booths and evenings at the grange hall. Slow work, but the farmers remember who showed up.

@organize_urban
on-arrival: urban_ethnic_fl += 2

Organizers who speak Yiddish, Italian, Polish and German knock on doors in the tenements. The machines have been there for decades, but they have never asked for anything other than votes.

@organize_industrial
on-arrival: industrial_fl += 2

In company towns where a man can be fired for reading the wrong newspaper, the work is quiet and dangerous. It is also the most loyal support the party has.

@organize_towns
on-arrival: small_town_fl += 2

Teachers, shopkeepers and ministers are not natural allies, but a patient organizer with a pamphlet on clean government and a cup of coffee can make progress.

@organize_none

The month passes without incident.
